WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:06.770
Central America is in the midst of what the
world recognizes as a humanitarian crisis.

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:11.960
Criminal gangs are taking over much of the
region and a civilian was more likely to be

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:16.590
murdered in one of these countries over the
last several years than to be killed in Iraq

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:19.350
during the height of the insurgency.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:24.050
Those gangs are recruiting young teenagers,
and if the teenager resists recruitment, will

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:28.230
threaten, injure or even kill the teenager
or family members.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:32.890
As a result, many teenagers and younger children
are choosing to leave their home countries

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:34.980
and seek asylum elsewhere in the region.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.441
They're going to Mexico, to Costa Rica, to
Nicaragua, and many of them are going to the

00:00:39.441 --> 00:00:40.700
United States.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:46.520
Since October 1st of last year, 52,000 children
unaccompanied by adults have been apprehended

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:47.680
by Border Patrol.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.790
And that's not counting the tens of thousands
of mothers who are bringing their children

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.309
into the United States to flee as well.

00:00:54.309 --> 00:00:59.239
Of course having so many people coming into
the country admittedly without papers is causing

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:00.890
a lot of controversy.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:04.600
Part of this is because they've strained the
existing system, which was built under the

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.070
Bush administration to deal with 6,000 to
8,000 kids coming across the border alone

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:11.289
every year, not 52,000.

00:01:11.289 --> 00:01:15.950
But part of it's because some would like the
border to be a forcefield, to automatically

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:19.899
refuse anyone who doesn't have the proper
papers from being able to set foot in the

00:01:19.899 --> 00:01:20.899
United States.

00:01:20.899 --> 00:01:24.350
But there are very good reasons that the border
isn't a forcefield.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:29.430
U.S. law has an established process so that
when someone comes across the border and doesn't

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:34.209
have papers but fears for their life there
can be a way to determine if they're eligible

00:01:34.209 --> 00:01:37.030
for asylum or another form of humanitarian
relief.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.749
But that's the system that's currently being
overwhelmed.

00:01:39.749 --> 00:01:44.079
So the question facing the United States right
now is how far are we willing to go in the

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:49.319
name of immigration enforcement without undermining
the humanitarian commitment that if you fear

00:01:49.319 --> 00:01:53.030
persecution and you escape to America, we
will try to find a place for you.

